WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.425
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:07.140
MATTHEW MOORE: Good
afternoon, everybody.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:10.250
Thank you all so much for
joining us today and being

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:12.360
here at this year's
Next conference

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:16.430
and making time on a Friday
afternoon to fill the house.

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:20.010
We're extremely excited to
see all of you here today.

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:22.670
To introduce myself,
my name is Matt Moore,

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:26.580
and I'm the product manager
on the Google Cloud Jobs API,

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:32.119
which was launched in
Alpha this last November.

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:36.530
And today, I'm really excited
to talk to you about something

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:41.690
that I'm extremely passionate
about, which is job search.

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:44.510
But first of all, in this
session, what we're going to do

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:47.690
is we're going to take
a behind-the-scenes look

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:50.260
at the Jobs API.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:52.480
We're going to provide
an update on what

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:55.750
we've been working on on the
API since our announcement

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:57.720
in November.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.970
And we're extremely
excited to be joined today

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:04.030
by the head of talent
management at Johnson &amp; Johnson

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:07.390
to hear about his
experiences deploying

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:10.590
the API to the Johnson
&amp; Johnson career site.

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:19.040
First of all, what is the
Google Cloud Jobs API?

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:22.370
Well, obviously, it's the first
vertical pre-trained machine

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:23.850
learning model from Google.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:24.670
That's clear.

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:26.570
Right?

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:29.900
But what does this really mean?

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:32.920
To answer that, let's
take a step back--

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:33.480
story time.

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:41.370
Here at Google, we started
with a simple question.

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:45.660
How could we create
a tenfold increase

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:47.490
in the number of
people that we hire?

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:53.440
We began by taking a deep
look at all of Google's hiring

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:54.940
channels.

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:59.350
And we found, to our surprise,
that the Google career

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:02.730
site, which you
see here, was one

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:07.090
of the most scalable
channels in that it

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:11.680
was severely under-utilized.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:14.320
So after analyzing user
behaviors and interactions

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:17.950
on the career site,
we realized if we

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:22.270
were able to make even a small
change to the career site

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:26.980
conversion rate, we'd be
making a material difference

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:31.330
to the bottom line, to
the number of people

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:35.130
that we hired every year.

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:37.060
And so we looked
for opportunities

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:39.280
where we could optimize.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:43.270
We ran countless experiments,
and we focused hard

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:46.450
on improving the career
site visitor experience,

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:51.100
looking for every way we could
drive an increase to conversion

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:56.500
and ultimately generate
high-quality hires.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:58.660
The resulting solutions,
which are still

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:00.730
in use on the Google
career site today--

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:03.670
feel free to take a
look as we are hiring--

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:08.870
became the first version of
the Google Cloud Jobs API.

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:10.370
So I'd like to take
this opportunity

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:13.380
to introduce Christian Posse,
our chief data scientist, who

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:14.840
is here to talk
about the problems

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:18.080
we've seen along the way and
what we've done to solve them.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:20.480
Please join me in welcoming
Christian to the stage.

00:03:24.461 --> 00:03:25.710
CHRISTIAN POSSE: Hi, everyone.

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:27.240
Can you hear me?

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:29.580
So yes, let's take a second
just to talk about one

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:32.830
of the main problems that the
Jobs API is trying to tackle.

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:36.310
So as you know, there's no
external writing job postings.

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:37.920
So very often, what
we're seeing are

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.350
companies which are
struggling and using

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:43.020
some kind of internal
language for describing

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:46.890
job titles that may not
be of use to job seekers.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:50.120
So you can see a few
of them up there.

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:50.970
They are real.

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:52.770
We have seen them
through the Jobs API.

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:54.360
They are not the
worst ones, and I'm

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:55.920
sure that if you
are in the industry,

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:58.860
you can come up
with weirder ones.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:02.090
On the other hand, job
seekers do not have a PhD

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:04.670
in terminology, and so
they cannot anticipate all

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:08.480
the possible ways that a
title can be expressed.

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:11.600
So even if you are a
recovery and asset protection

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:16.110
specialist, you may never find
these rec and asset prot level

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:17.630
one jobs.

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:21.240
Because how on earth do you
know it's spelled this way?

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:25.315
So the Google Cloud Jobs API
is like a translation engine--

00:04:25.315 --> 00:04:27.590
a Rosetta Stone, we
like to call it--

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:30.440
that helps match the
employer's interest

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:32.690
with a job seeker's interest.

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:36.200
So now if you are trying to
fill this rec and asset prot

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:38.720
level one position,
the API is going

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:41.660
to help you surface a job
to the job seekers which

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:43.760
are best suited for it.

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:45.900
By the way, the Rosetta
Stone, if you remember,

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:51.870
is a stone that was rediscovered
by the French in Egypt in 1799.

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:55.170
It contains three translations
of the same decree.

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:57.550
At the bottom, it's
in Ancient Greek.

00:04:57.550 --> 00:04:59.250
It was known at the time.

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:04.370
The top one was Ancient Egyptian
hieroglyphs that were unknown.

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:06.690
And thanks to the
stone, 20 years later,

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:10.250
a French scholar was able
to translate or decipher

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:11.660
the hieroglyphs.

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:13.850
So I like to think about
the job seeker queries

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:16.777
like the Ancient Greek, who
kind of know what's going on.

00:05:16.777 --> 00:05:18.860
You have the job posting
titles, who have no idea.

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:20.270
These are the hieroglyphs.

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:23.650
Essentially, the API
is the Rosetta Stone.

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:27.260
But the best way to fill a
problem is to see it in action.

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:29.530
So let's look at
the search behavior

00:05:29.530 --> 00:05:32.440
on the previous version of
the Johnson &amp; Johnson career

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:33.730
website.

00:05:33.730 --> 00:05:35.860
And we're going to take
a very simple query,

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:39.890
like searching for an admin
assistant role in New York.

00:05:39.890 --> 00:05:42.330
What could go wrong?

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:46.200
Well, if you look at the
results, it's a bit surprising.

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:48.750
They are all mismatches,
and there are

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:50.950
a couple of reasons for that.

00:05:50.950 --> 00:05:55.230
The first one is the realization
that very often job titles

00:05:55.230 --> 00:06:00.770
are the combinations of two
or three very generic terms.

00:06:00.770 --> 00:06:03.820
Independently, each
term is very vague.

00:06:03.820 --> 00:06:05.740
But when you put
them together, they

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:08.270
create a very specific meaning.

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:12.570
And this makes any keyword-based
search very challenging.

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:13.070
OK.

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:14.720
You saw that problem.

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:16.680
There are more issues.

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:19.910
One is that an admin
assistant is a supporting

00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:23.090
role for senior positions.

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:25.570
So it will tend to be mentioned
in the job descriptions

00:06:25.570 --> 00:06:27.550
of the more senior role.

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:29.200
And now you have
an increased risk

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:31.090
of returning the senior
role in the search

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:33.850
results for the admin
assistant query,

00:06:33.850 --> 00:06:37.210
just because you had the
hit on the secondary role

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:40.014
in the job descriptions
for the senior role.

00:06:40.014 --> 00:06:41.680
And it's what happens
here, essentially.

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:45.700
If you look here, all of these
drugs are for the seniors.

00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:50.160
And by doing so, you're missing
the intent of the job seeker.

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:53.770
So the API is attacking
this issue and many others,

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:56.020
and we will see a bit
more in the future.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:59.210
But how does it work?

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:01.790
So at the core of
the Google Cloud

00:07:01.790 --> 00:07:06.620
Jobs APIs are data models
that encode our knowledge

00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:12.110
about occupations, skills,
educations, locations,

00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:16.070
companies, employment
type, and more.

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:18.530
What you're seeing here
is a visual representation

00:07:18.530 --> 00:07:22.420
of one of our data models, which
is the title or occupation data

00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:23.949
model.

00:07:23.949 --> 00:07:26.490
So once again, just imagine that
you are at night and looking

00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:27.070
in the sky.

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:29.520
Try to do that tonight, but
good luck in San Francisco.

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:30.720
There is a fog.

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:33.420
So go back home and
look at the sky.

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:37.596
Imagine that every star
you're looking at is a title.

00:07:37.596 --> 00:07:41.260
Related titles are
close stars in the sky.

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:44.450
And like stars
concentrated in galaxies,

00:07:44.450 --> 00:07:46.490
titles that belong to
the same job categories

00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:49.910
will tend to
concentrate in clusters.

00:07:49.910 --> 00:07:53.150
So if you look at our skies,
we have some exciting galaxies

00:07:53.150 --> 00:07:56.210
here, like the accounting
and finance, or the business

00:07:56.210 --> 00:07:59.840
operations, or the art
of fashions and design.

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:02.210
Each galaxy is made of
thousands of titles,

00:08:02.210 --> 00:08:06.500
and we understand
around 250,000 titles.

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:07.900
Yes.

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:09.610
But much more
interesting is if you

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:11.360
look at the structure
within the galaxies.

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:13.520
There's a fine
structure that encodes

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:17.330
the type of similarities
between those titles.

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:17.830
OK.

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:22.650
So we are in the age
of space travel, right?

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:25.350
So maybe we can go visit
some of these stars.

00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:26.749
You want to do that?

00:08:26.749 --> 00:08:29.040
I don't even know why I'm
asking you, because in a way,

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:30.040
you don't have a choice.

00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:30.970
Doors are closed.

00:08:30.970 --> 00:08:34.419
So put your seat belts
on and enjoy the ride.

00:08:34.419 --> 00:08:36.070
So let's look at the video.

00:08:36.070 --> 00:08:39.870
So our journey is going to
start by visiting the business

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:42.919
development manager star.

00:08:42.919 --> 00:08:46.570
So what we want to see with this
video is a couple of things.

00:08:46.570 --> 00:08:49.400
I will tell you what
you would have visited.

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:52.150
So if you're in the business
development manager stars,

00:08:52.150 --> 00:08:54.870
you're going to see that
in the neighborhood space

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:58.390
there are very small
nuances of that title.

00:08:58.390 --> 00:08:59.080
Oh, thank you.

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:02.070
Finally, we're kicking off.

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:03.060
Excellent.

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:04.470
There we go.

00:09:04.470 --> 00:09:06.930
We are in the advertising
and marketing galaxy.

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:09.600
So the first thing you can see
here in the close by space--

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:11.522
we are capturing
very small nuances,

00:09:11.522 --> 00:09:13.980
like a business development
manager or it can be a business

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:15.180
development director.

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:18.510
So we have these nuances between
a manager and a director.

00:09:18.510 --> 00:09:21.680
You have a business
development marketing manager.

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:24.420
We also capture a lot of
vertical video manager role,

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:27.060
like a government business
development manager,

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:32.550
IT development manager, or
retail business development

00:09:32.550 --> 00:09:33.340
manager.

00:09:33.340 --> 00:09:36.150
So those are small
nuances we capture.

00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:37.780
But a business
development manager

00:09:37.780 --> 00:09:39.910
can be also expressed
in many different ways,

00:09:39.910 --> 00:09:43.480
like for example, a brand
development manager.

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:45.310
So we just visited
a nearby space.

00:09:45.310 --> 00:09:46.910
We went a bit further.

00:09:46.910 --> 00:09:50.470
And you can see here,
we exemplify a few more

00:09:50.470 --> 00:09:52.390
alternatives of how a
business development

00:09:52.390 --> 00:09:54.070
manager is being expressed.

00:09:54.070 --> 00:09:56.650
It can be mentioned as a
director of brand strategy,

00:09:56.650 --> 00:10:01.630
or a strategic planner, partner
relationship manager, even

00:10:01.630 --> 00:10:06.370
a chief marketing officer or
an account marketing executive.

00:10:06.370 --> 00:10:06.910
OK.

00:10:06.910 --> 00:10:08.535
So now we're going
to switch, and we're

00:10:08.535 --> 00:10:10.400
going to go to a far,
far away galaxy, which

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:14.150
happens to be the transportation
and logistic galaxy.

00:10:14.150 --> 00:10:17.570
And we're going to visit a star
which is the truck driver star.

00:10:17.570 --> 00:10:19.570
So it takes a little
while, because really we're

00:10:19.570 --> 00:10:21.620
going to go across the universe.

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:24.130
There we go.

00:10:24.130 --> 00:10:25.260
And look and see.

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:28.930
Again, we have tons of nuances
around the truck driver.

00:10:28.930 --> 00:10:31.540
So in the nearby space, you
capture things like a delivery

00:10:31.540 --> 00:10:36.670
driver, or a class-A CDL
driver, a 7.5-ton driver,

00:10:36.670 --> 00:10:42.070
or things like car transporter,
or tow truck coordinator.

00:10:42.070 --> 00:10:43.840
But the most important
thing to realize

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:46.540
is that there are
strong differences

00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:48.430
between those roles.

00:10:48.430 --> 00:10:52.150
For example, heavyweight and
lightweight truck driving

00:10:52.150 --> 00:10:55.060
have very different
driving requirements,

00:10:55.060 --> 00:10:57.160
in terms of driver license.

00:10:57.160 --> 00:11:00.810
So you never want to return
heavyweight truck driver roles

00:11:00.810 --> 00:11:03.190
to a lightweight
truck driver query,

00:11:03.190 --> 00:11:05.230
because there's a mismatch.

00:11:05.230 --> 00:11:07.890
Finally, we're going to go
into the admin assistant role

00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:09.730
that we saw a bit
earlier, which happens

00:11:09.730 --> 00:11:12.010
to be in the administrative
and office galaxy, which

00:11:12.010 --> 00:11:13.300
is a good one.

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:14.170
Same pattern here.

00:11:14.170 --> 00:11:16.130
You can see that we have
all the possible nuances.

00:11:16.130 --> 00:11:17.350
So for example,
an admin assistant

00:11:17.350 --> 00:11:19.933
can be a personal assistant, an
office assistant, a secretary,

00:11:19.933 --> 00:11:22.990
administration
coordinator, and so on.

00:11:22.990 --> 00:11:25.450
We again capture a
vertical admin assistant,

00:11:25.450 --> 00:11:29.029
like faculty admin assistant
or store admin assistant.

00:11:29.029 --> 00:11:30.820
We capture also different
seniority levels,

00:11:30.820 --> 00:11:34.550
like senior admin assistant,
or admin assistant one, two,

00:11:34.550 --> 00:11:36.660
or three.

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:37.320
OK.

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:38.690
So we're done with our space.

00:11:38.690 --> 00:11:40.320
I hope you're not too dizzy.

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:42.162
But as you can see,
it was fast travel--

00:11:42.162 --> 00:11:43.620
some hold-up at
the very beginning,

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:47.630
but we did this travel, and
we never spent quality time

00:11:47.630 --> 00:11:49.230
at one of these stars.

00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:52.800
So what we're going to do now is
take a break, take a vacation,

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:54.810
and revisit one of these stars--

00:11:54.810 --> 00:11:57.920
the exciting business
development manager one--

00:11:57.920 --> 00:12:00.190
and try to explore
it in more depth.

00:12:00.190 --> 00:12:02.340
So what we said earlier
is we understand a lot

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:03.480
about this title.

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:06.300
The first thing is we understand
all the possible synonyms that

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:09.330
can be expressed,
especially also slang ones.

00:12:09.330 --> 00:12:11.360
So if you are in
the biz dev world,

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:12.910
you may spell
something like bizdev

00:12:12.910 --> 00:12:16.050
in one single word, where we
capture business development

00:12:16.050 --> 00:12:17.220
in that case.

00:12:17.220 --> 00:12:19.050
You can use abbreviations.

00:12:19.050 --> 00:12:21.210
There, too, those are captured.

00:12:21.210 --> 00:12:22.710
You just saw earlier,
we understand

00:12:22.710 --> 00:12:25.210
a lot of the related roles.

00:12:25.210 --> 00:12:27.570
We also have relationships
and strength of relationships

00:12:27.570 --> 00:12:28.680
between those roles.

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:30.980
Those are captured.

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:33.680
Earlier you saw that this role
belongs to the advertising

00:12:33.680 --> 00:12:35.300
and marketing galaxy,
but actually it

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:36.890
belongs to more
galaxies than that.

00:12:36.890 --> 00:12:39.450
It also belongs to the sales
galaxy and the management

00:12:39.450 --> 00:12:39.950
galaxy.

00:12:39.950 --> 00:12:42.630
You want to capture all of
these facets of the diamond

00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:45.644
that a business development
manager role is.

00:12:45.644 --> 00:12:47.060
And all of these
relationships, we

00:12:47.060 --> 00:12:49.010
have them with
confidence scores,

00:12:49.010 --> 00:12:53.010
so you know the strength
of their relationships.

00:12:53.010 --> 00:12:56.690
Finally, we also understand
what are the skills related

00:12:56.690 --> 00:12:58.280
to that role.

00:12:58.280 --> 00:13:00.410
And since we're talking
about the skills,

00:13:00.410 --> 00:13:04.449
why don't we do a small, deep
dive into our skills ontology?

00:13:04.449 --> 00:13:06.740
And we're going to use the
business development manager

00:13:06.740 --> 00:13:11.130
role to exemplify what
we're trying to build.

00:13:11.130 --> 00:13:13.910
So what you're seeing here
is a simplified snippet

00:13:13.910 --> 00:13:17.180
of our skills data model related
to the business development

00:13:17.180 --> 00:13:18.830
manager case.

00:13:18.830 --> 00:13:22.200
So the first thing is if you are
a business development manager,

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:25.220
you must have business
development skills.

00:13:25.220 --> 00:13:27.164
That's kind of a
duh moment, right?

00:13:27.164 --> 00:13:28.580
But actually, it's
a critical one,

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:31.310
because at this point in
time we can start reasoning

00:13:31.310 --> 00:13:33.610
about what it means.

00:13:33.610 --> 00:13:37.140
Business development
skills-- it's

00:13:37.140 --> 00:13:38.700
a collection of
different skills,

00:13:38.700 --> 00:13:42.630
such as brand building,
finding business opportunities,

00:13:42.630 --> 00:13:46.110
relationship building, sales
skills, strategy management

00:13:46.110 --> 00:13:47.302
skills.

00:13:47.302 --> 00:13:48.760
But if you have
this set of skills,

00:13:48.760 --> 00:13:51.520
you'd better have good
communication and interpersonal

00:13:51.520 --> 00:13:53.082
skills.

00:13:53.082 --> 00:13:54.790
So what you're seeing
here is essentially

00:13:54.790 --> 00:13:58.014
we have a graph of skills
with different types

00:13:58.014 --> 00:14:00.430
of relationships, which are
encoded by the different types

00:14:00.430 --> 00:14:02.012
of arrows here.

00:14:02.012 --> 00:14:03.720
So some skills are
part of larger skills,

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:05.303
some skills are
collections of skills,

00:14:05.303 --> 00:14:07.330
some skills are
prerequisites of skills,

00:14:07.330 --> 00:14:09.437
and some skills are
related to other skills.

00:14:09.437 --> 00:14:11.270
For example, the business
development skills

00:14:11.270 --> 00:14:15.215
are related to strategy
partnership development.

00:14:15.215 --> 00:14:17.590
And more importantly, also we
understand the relationship

00:14:17.590 --> 00:14:21.100
between occupations
skills and education.

00:14:21.100 --> 00:14:24.010
So we did earlier the hop from
business development manager

00:14:24.010 --> 00:14:26.290
to the business
development skills.

00:14:26.290 --> 00:14:30.310
Business development belongs
to the larger business skills,

00:14:30.310 --> 00:14:34.030
which is commonly associated
with an MBA degree, which

00:14:34.030 --> 00:14:36.980
is included here.

00:14:36.980 --> 00:14:37.570
OK.

00:14:37.570 --> 00:14:41.500
So we talked about the
mismatch between job seekers

00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:42.460
and employers.

00:14:42.460 --> 00:14:45.050
We talked about all these
beautiful data models.

00:14:45.050 --> 00:14:46.940
Well, how do we bridge the two?

00:14:46.940 --> 00:14:50.500
And this is where machine
learning comes into play.

00:14:50.500 --> 00:14:54.450
So essentially,
machine learning helps

00:14:54.450 --> 00:14:58.530
us extract content on both sides
from the employer and the job

00:14:58.530 --> 00:15:02.100
seeker and map the
content to the data

00:15:02.100 --> 00:15:04.960
model with great accuracy.

00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:06.840
Once we have done the
mapping, essentially we

00:15:06.840 --> 00:15:09.510
can leverage Google
search technology

00:15:09.510 --> 00:15:13.220
for retrieving and
ranking search results.

00:15:13.220 --> 00:15:16.250
So here on the left,
you see the employer.

00:15:16.250 --> 00:15:18.470
Whenever a job
posting is inserted

00:15:18.470 --> 00:15:21.330
in the private tenant on
the Google Cloud platform,

00:15:21.330 --> 00:15:25.430
we parse the job posting,
and we structure it.

00:15:25.430 --> 00:15:28.220
So we're going to use now
natural language processing

00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:31.790
and machine learning for
essentially building structure.

00:15:31.790 --> 00:15:33.770
For example, the
first thing we do

00:15:33.770 --> 00:15:37.970
is we automatically
segment the job description

00:15:37.970 --> 00:15:42.300
into its main constituents,
which are responsibilities,

00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:45.140
company descriptions,
requirements,

00:15:45.140 --> 00:15:47.060
and other subsections.

00:15:47.060 --> 00:15:50.900
We also extract two dozens of
what we call Nuggets, like,

00:15:50.900 --> 00:15:53.140
for example, all the leaves--

00:15:53.140 --> 00:15:56.660
parental leaves, sick
leaves, vacation.

00:15:56.660 --> 00:15:59.120
We extract all the
benefits if they

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:01.100
are present on the job posting.

00:16:01.100 --> 00:16:05.330
We extract things like shift
flexibility, life insurance,

00:16:05.330 --> 00:16:08.190
401k, tuition reimbursement.

00:16:08.190 --> 00:16:10.910
So we extract these data,
and we normalize them,

00:16:10.910 --> 00:16:13.010
and we surface them.

00:16:13.010 --> 00:16:15.140
We also extract and
normalize the skills

00:16:15.140 --> 00:16:17.399
from the Requirements section.

00:16:17.399 --> 00:16:19.440
Also something you saw
earlier-- we automatically

00:16:19.440 --> 00:16:21.110
categorize the job
posting into one

00:16:21.110 --> 00:16:23.775
of these broad job
categories, our galaxies

00:16:23.775 --> 00:16:25.590
from a bit earlier.

00:16:25.590 --> 00:16:28.640
So we created a very
large set of metadata

00:16:28.640 --> 00:16:31.510
that we're going to
leverage in the search.

00:16:31.510 --> 00:16:33.260
On the right side, you
see the job seeker.

00:16:33.260 --> 00:16:37.120
So when the Jobs API gets
the job seeker query,

00:16:37.120 --> 00:16:39.120
we do the same work.

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:41.520
We try to identify if there
is a title, employment

00:16:41.520 --> 00:16:45.190
type, company, skills,
education, statement.

00:16:45.190 --> 00:16:47.160
Pretty much anything
that moves, we

00:16:47.160 --> 00:16:49.530
try to detect it
and normalize it,

00:16:49.530 --> 00:16:52.770
so that we can now match to
the rich metadata of the job

00:16:52.770 --> 00:16:56.000
posting we just talked about.

00:16:56.000 --> 00:16:59.920
So maybe we can take a minute
to go visit one of these data

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:02.780
processes that we have
built. And the one example

00:17:02.780 --> 00:17:08.230
maybe we can use is
our title standardizer.

00:17:08.230 --> 00:17:14.410
As you know, job posting
titles are very, very noisy.

00:17:14.410 --> 00:17:16.550
I think I will just
give you an example.

00:17:16.550 --> 00:17:18.880
I think in the millions and
millions of job postings

00:17:18.880 --> 00:17:22.690
that we have analyzed, we
find that actually the number

00:17:22.690 --> 00:17:25.140
of unique strings that you
can extract in terms of titles

00:17:25.140 --> 00:17:26.859
on job postings,
it's pretty much

00:17:26.859 --> 00:17:29.960
just reduced your set by 75%.

00:17:29.960 --> 00:17:32.410
So essentially, if you have
10 million job postings,

00:17:32.410 --> 00:17:35.409
you end up with 2.5
million job titles

00:17:35.409 --> 00:17:36.450
which are unique strings.

00:17:36.450 --> 00:17:37.390
It's very noisy.

00:17:37.390 --> 00:17:38.560
Why?

00:17:38.560 --> 00:17:39.850
Because there are ads.

00:17:39.850 --> 00:17:42.970
And so often you find
content that does not

00:17:42.970 --> 00:17:44.680
describe the occupation.

00:17:44.680 --> 00:17:49.270
You find things like location,
company name, employment type,

00:17:49.270 --> 00:17:52.630
but you find things also
like advertising jargon--

00:17:52.630 --> 00:17:55.870
needed, wanted, grand opening.

00:17:55.870 --> 00:17:58.170
And sometimes you find
extremely useful information

00:17:58.170 --> 00:18:01.650
like administrative
jargon, like a reference ID

00:18:01.650 --> 00:18:03.210
on the job posting.

00:18:03.210 --> 00:18:07.140
So the first thing that a Jobs
API does is clean up the mess.

00:18:07.140 --> 00:18:10.320
And then we standardize
the remaining content.

00:18:10.320 --> 00:18:13.080
So if you have acronyms,
abbreviations, we also

00:18:13.080 --> 00:18:15.360
do contextual disambiguation.

00:18:15.360 --> 00:18:18.090
And finally, also, we extract
the skills and specialties

00:18:18.090 --> 00:18:22.330
from the job postings and
add that to the title.

00:18:22.330 --> 00:18:25.430
At this point in time, we
built high-dimensional vector

00:18:25.430 --> 00:18:28.200
representations of those titles.

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:31.470
The dimension of the vector
is essentially the terms

00:18:31.470 --> 00:18:33.720
and expressions that
we have gathered

00:18:33.720 --> 00:18:38.130
from the titles and the
skills extracted from millions

00:18:38.130 --> 00:18:40.110
of job postings across
hundreds of thousands

00:18:40.110 --> 00:18:42.490
of career websites.

00:18:42.490 --> 00:18:44.000
So now we can use
machine learning.

00:18:44.000 --> 00:18:47.210
At this point in time,
we built a classifier.

00:18:47.210 --> 00:18:48.890
It's an ensemble
classifier that is

00:18:48.890 --> 00:18:51.380
a collection of
natural-language-based

00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:55.100
processing classifiers
and the neural nets.

00:18:55.100 --> 00:18:59.450
And the classifier will attempt
at mapping the statement that

00:18:59.450 --> 00:19:02.690
you are feeding it--
so this title string--

00:19:02.690 --> 00:19:08.290
into zero, one, or more
occupations in our title data

00:19:08.290 --> 00:19:10.330
model.

00:19:10.330 --> 00:19:11.980
Why zero, you ask?

00:19:11.980 --> 00:19:13.860
I'm glad you asked.

00:19:13.860 --> 00:19:17.460
Well, Princess of Darkness--

00:19:17.460 --> 00:19:20.530
it's a title we have seen.

00:19:20.530 --> 00:19:23.850
And I use that all the
time, so not literal.

00:19:23.850 --> 00:19:26.460
No, Princess of Darkness.

00:19:26.460 --> 00:19:30.360
So unless you have
some extra information

00:19:30.360 --> 00:19:33.570
that you have extracted from
a job posting, like skills,

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:37.530
we must be able to say, I have
no clue what to do with that.

00:19:37.530 --> 00:19:40.730
On the other hand, very often,
especially on the query side,

00:19:40.730 --> 00:19:45.710
especially also with the blue
collars and those skills,

00:19:45.710 --> 00:19:47.460
you don't find very
specific titles.

00:19:47.460 --> 00:19:49.640
Titles tend to be pretty vague.

00:19:49.640 --> 00:19:52.550
So you must be able-- like
retail sales or team members

00:19:52.550 --> 00:19:54.890
or I want to work
in warehousing.

00:19:54.890 --> 00:19:58.770
You must be able to map
the query or the titles

00:19:58.770 --> 00:20:00.800
to not a single node
into the ontology

00:20:00.800 --> 00:20:04.135
but to a multitude of them.

00:20:04.135 --> 00:20:06.010
And we do that with a
confidence score, which

00:20:06.010 --> 00:20:08.170
means that whenever
we do the mapping,

00:20:08.170 --> 00:20:10.210
we have a sense that
the mapping was done

00:20:10.210 --> 00:20:13.250
without ambiguity-- or
maybe with some ambiguity,

00:20:13.250 --> 00:20:17.800
and we take care of that
also in the search results.

00:20:17.800 --> 00:20:20.410
So that concludes
our little, deep dive

00:20:20.410 --> 00:20:22.480
into the title standardizer.

00:20:22.480 --> 00:20:25.750
We have similar standardizers
for education, skills,

00:20:25.750 --> 00:20:26.546
and others.

00:20:26.546 --> 00:20:27.920
But I think at
this point in time

00:20:27.920 --> 00:20:29.710
you're much more
excited to see how

00:20:29.710 --> 00:20:31.100
these surface into the product.

00:20:31.100 --> 00:20:33.670
So I'm going to
hand back to Matt

00:20:33.670 --> 00:20:36.320
to see how our customers
are using the API today.

00:20:36.320 --> 00:20:36.820
Thank you.

00:20:44.269 --> 00:20:45.310
MATTHEW MOORE: All right.

00:20:45.310 --> 00:20:47.550
Thank you, Christian.

00:20:47.550 --> 00:20:53.810
So our goal is to bring
this next wave of computing

00:20:53.810 --> 00:20:56.540
to the entire talent industry.

00:20:56.540 --> 00:20:59.150
Fundamentally
changing how employers

00:20:59.150 --> 00:21:05.730
are able to attract, connect
with, and retain talent,

00:21:05.730 --> 00:21:09.240
job search, if you
think about it,

00:21:09.240 --> 00:21:13.670
is just the first step
in this API's journey.

00:21:13.670 --> 00:21:17.570
I mean, almost every business
starts their hiring processes

00:21:17.570 --> 00:21:19.370
with job search.

00:21:19.370 --> 00:21:23.060
And as we found,
once optimized, it's

00:21:23.060 --> 00:21:28.790
a very powerful tool
in your hiring arsenal.

00:21:28.790 --> 00:21:32.750
So by working with existing
independent software vendors

00:21:32.750 --> 00:21:37.160
and job platforms, we aspire
to create turnkey access

00:21:37.160 --> 00:21:39.620
to the API.

00:21:39.620 --> 00:21:42.260
We want to provide a better
job search experience

00:21:42.260 --> 00:21:44.960
for all employers
and candidates.

00:21:44.960 --> 00:21:46.990
Because let's face it--

00:21:46.990 --> 00:21:51.130
hiring the right people is one
of the most important things

00:21:51.130 --> 00:21:52.180
your company needs to do.

00:21:54.860 --> 00:21:58.610
To date we've announced two
core use cases, both of which

00:21:58.610 --> 00:22:02.240
are running in a closed alpha
program in North America.

00:22:02.240 --> 00:22:07.380
The first one is
powering job boards.

00:22:07.380 --> 00:22:10.320
For the job board case,
we are pleased to share

00:22:10.320 --> 00:22:13.710
that CareerBuilder is
nearing a production release,

00:22:13.710 --> 00:22:15.840
and ongoing testing
and improvements

00:22:15.840 --> 00:22:17.300
continue to date with Dice.com.

00:22:20.750 --> 00:22:24.230
Our second and extremely
important use case

00:22:24.230 --> 00:22:29.751
is powering talent networks
and company career sites.

00:22:29.751 --> 00:22:31.750
We're also pleased to
announce for CareerBuilder

00:22:31.750 --> 00:22:34.510
that they've brought
over 20 companies

00:22:34.510 --> 00:22:37.730
online with the API in
their talent network,

00:22:37.730 --> 00:22:43.380
including CIOX Health,
GameStop, and Lucas Group,

00:22:43.380 --> 00:22:47.490
helping thousands of job seekers
find their next job going

00:22:47.490 --> 00:22:49.430
forward.

00:22:49.430 --> 00:22:53.090
Additionally, the Cloud
Jobs API continues

00:22:53.090 --> 00:22:57.800
to power job search on the
Google and FedEx career sites.

00:22:57.800 --> 00:23:00.670
And later this month,
Jibe will be onboarding

00:23:00.670 --> 00:23:05.660
HealthSouth, the largest owner
and operator of inpatient rehab

00:23:05.660 --> 00:23:09.110
hospitals in the United
States, and Eaton,

00:23:09.110 --> 00:23:13.010
a multinational power
management company.

00:23:13.010 --> 00:23:16.880
Specifically for Google and
our online hiring efforts,

00:23:16.880 --> 00:23:21.680
our career site handles several
million queries each month.

00:23:21.680 --> 00:23:27.920
And we've seen a 28% increase
in candidate success rate

00:23:27.920 --> 00:23:33.860
from apply to hire since our
journey with the API began.

00:23:33.860 --> 00:23:36.860
And last but definitely
not least, we're

00:23:36.860 --> 00:23:38.570
pleased to be joined
here today by one

00:23:38.570 --> 00:23:41.930
of our newly onboarded
customers, Johnson &amp; Johnson.

00:23:41.930 --> 00:23:44.160
So please join me in
welcoming up to the stage

00:23:44.160 --> 00:23:46.040
Sjoerd Gehring,
Global Vice President

00:23:46.040 --> 00:23:49.130
of Talent Acquisition at Johnson
&amp; Johnson, and Joe Essenfeld,

00:23:49.130 --> 00:23:50.870
CEO and founder of Jibe.

00:23:50.870 --> 00:23:52.368
Welcome up.

00:23:52.368 --> 00:23:55.242
[APPLAUSE]

00:23:59.313 --> 00:24:00.520
Come on.

00:24:00.520 --> 00:24:02.360
There we go.

00:24:02.360 --> 00:24:02.860
All right.

00:24:02.860 --> 00:24:05.290
Thanks for joining us
here today, guys, and it's

00:24:05.290 --> 00:24:09.370
great to have both of you here
to talk about the Jobs API.

00:24:09.370 --> 00:24:11.910
So, Sjoerd, I was wondering
to kind of start us off,

00:24:11.910 --> 00:24:15.070
could you talk a little bit
about Johnson &amp; Johnson's

00:24:15.070 --> 00:24:17.950
hiring processes and some
of the challenges you face?

00:24:17.950 --> 00:24:19.325
SJOERD GEHRING:
Absolutely, Matt,

00:24:19.325 --> 00:24:21.040
and it's a pleasure
to be here today.

00:24:21.040 --> 00:24:25.690
At Johnson &amp; Johnson, we fill
about 25,000 positions a year.

00:24:25.690 --> 00:24:29.410
And as a company, we have
a goal in the next decades

00:24:29.410 --> 00:24:31.650
to find a cure for cancer.

00:24:31.650 --> 00:24:34.630
So we're heavily investing in
our oncology and hematology

00:24:34.630 --> 00:24:36.130
businesses.

00:24:36.130 --> 00:24:39.880
Currently, we have approximately
350 roles open today

00:24:39.880 --> 00:24:42.790
in the oncology and
hematology space,

00:24:42.790 --> 00:24:44.620
primarily in the
research and development,

00:24:44.620 --> 00:24:47.410
and various commercial roles.

00:24:47.410 --> 00:24:51.100
Given the fact that these
roles are so niche and so

00:24:51.100 --> 00:24:53.650
hard to fill, we're
having a real issue

00:24:53.650 --> 00:24:55.870
filling these positions
simply because we're tapping

00:24:55.870 --> 00:24:58.420
into a very small talent pool.

00:24:58.420 --> 00:25:02.020
And our current
approach is really not

00:25:02.020 --> 00:25:04.480
getting us to the hiring goals
that we need fast enough,

00:25:04.480 --> 00:25:07.900
so we're always looking for
innovations related to job

00:25:07.900 --> 00:25:09.849
search and hiring practices.

00:25:09.849 --> 00:25:10.640
MATTHEW MOORE: Wow.

00:25:10.640 --> 00:25:14.710
So you know, when you first
started chatting with Joe

00:25:14.710 --> 00:25:17.320
about the machine
learning and the API,

00:25:17.320 --> 00:25:21.415
what was it that
caught your attention

00:25:21.415 --> 00:25:23.290
as a possible way you
could use this to solve

00:25:23.290 --> 00:25:24.790
some of those challenges?

00:25:24.790 --> 00:25:24.900
SJOERD GEHRING: Yeah.

00:25:24.900 --> 00:25:27.460
We've been interested in
machine learning for a few years

00:25:27.460 --> 00:25:29.050
now, really because
we believe that it

00:25:29.050 --> 00:25:32.620
can enable us to much better
identify and hire talent

00:25:32.620 --> 00:25:33.700
faster.

00:25:33.700 --> 00:25:37.030
And one of the things that we
need to do a much better job of

00:25:37.030 --> 00:25:40.360
is to proactively
identify and match

00:25:40.360 --> 00:25:43.690
people with the skills and
interest in the oncology space

00:25:43.690 --> 00:25:45.640
to our various open positions.

00:25:45.640 --> 00:25:48.220
And we feel that the
Jobs API can actually

00:25:48.220 --> 00:25:49.660
make a real difference here.

00:25:49.660 --> 00:25:51.260
MATTHEW MOORE:
And I understand--

00:25:51.260 --> 00:25:53.740
I may have had a previous look--

00:25:53.740 --> 00:25:55.780
that you and Joe have
brought some examples

00:25:55.780 --> 00:25:57.130
to share with us today.

00:25:57.130 --> 00:25:57.550
SJOERD GEHRING: We have.

00:25:57.550 --> 00:25:59.883
So we prepared three different
examples for the audience

00:25:59.883 --> 00:26:01.010
today.

00:26:01.010 --> 00:26:05.320
The first one is a search query
around oncological research

00:26:05.320 --> 00:26:08.080
and in the location
the United States.

00:26:08.080 --> 00:26:10.480
And what you see on the
left side of this slide

00:26:10.480 --> 00:26:16.480
is our previous keyword-based
search technology.

00:26:16.480 --> 00:26:20.920
And on the right side you see
an example using Google search.

00:26:20.920 --> 00:26:23.480
And as you can imagine,
on the left side,

00:26:23.480 --> 00:26:25.270
a job seeker would
actually be quite

00:26:25.270 --> 00:26:29.290
discouraged having searched
for oncological research

00:26:29.290 --> 00:26:33.010
and not seeing any
oncology-specific positions

00:26:33.010 --> 00:26:37.390
in the top search results
that the search yielded.

00:26:37.390 --> 00:26:39.790
On the right-hand side, where
you see our Google search

00:26:39.790 --> 00:26:42.400
results, is that Google
search is actually

00:26:42.400 --> 00:26:44.920
using the understanding
of language

00:26:44.920 --> 00:26:51.880
to return much
more relevant jobs,

00:26:51.880 --> 00:26:57.470
even when it doesn't use the
exact keyword in these jobs.

00:26:57.470 --> 00:26:59.410
The other thing that
you see in this result

00:26:59.410 --> 00:27:02.860
is that the Google
search yields fewer

00:27:02.860 --> 00:27:05.260
and much more
quality and relevant

00:27:05.260 --> 00:27:09.834
search results over the before
picture, which really saves job

00:27:09.834 --> 00:27:11.000
seekers quite a bit of time.

00:27:13.690 --> 00:27:16.780
The second example is
around a search query

00:27:16.780 --> 00:27:19.510
around microbiologists.

00:27:19.510 --> 00:27:24.190
And what you see here is
that although the first two

00:27:24.190 --> 00:27:28.480
examples are pretty similar,
further down in the list

00:27:28.480 --> 00:27:33.040
you start again to see Google
search returning much more

00:27:33.040 --> 00:27:39.500
relevant job search
results for job seekers.

00:27:39.500 --> 00:27:41.860
And last but not least,
the third example

00:27:41.860 --> 00:27:44.380
here is around internships
in New York City.

00:27:44.380 --> 00:27:47.530
We get an enormous amount
of job search queries

00:27:47.530 --> 00:27:50.350
around internships with
J&amp;J. And I actually

00:27:50.350 --> 00:27:53.080
thought this was a particularly
interesting example, because

00:27:53.080 --> 00:27:56.530
in the before
picture you see four

00:27:56.530 --> 00:28:02.560
results in the search, three of
which aren't really relevant.

00:28:02.560 --> 00:28:06.040
And the after picture there,
11 relevant internship

00:28:06.040 --> 00:28:11.470
results in the New
York City area--

00:28:11.470 --> 00:28:13.660
much more relevant for
job seekers, again,

00:28:13.660 --> 00:28:16.240
using Google search.

00:28:16.240 --> 00:28:17.170
MATTHEW MOORE: Yes.

00:28:17.170 --> 00:28:19.660
First of all, thank you so
much for being here and sharing

00:28:19.660 --> 00:28:21.280
these examples with us.

00:28:21.280 --> 00:28:23.830
We're really excited to
continue to work with you

00:28:23.830 --> 00:28:27.310
as you move forward with hiring.

00:28:27.310 --> 00:28:30.880
So, Joe, just having
heard from Sjoerd, could

00:28:30.880 --> 00:28:33.550
you maybe tell us a little
bit about the API onboarding

00:28:33.550 --> 00:28:36.960
process with Johnson &amp; Johnson?

00:28:36.960 --> 00:28:39.080
JOE ESSENFELD: J&amp;J
was the second company

00:28:39.080 --> 00:28:43.202
to be onboarded on the API,
and Jibe powers career sites.

00:28:43.202 --> 00:28:44.660
So when we're going
into production

00:28:44.660 --> 00:28:47.450
with jobs for a global
company like J&amp;J,

00:28:47.450 --> 00:28:48.980
one of our concerns
are, how do you

00:28:48.980 --> 00:28:51.014
deal with that with a
closed alpha program?

00:28:51.014 --> 00:28:52.430
You're putting
jobs in production.

00:28:52.430 --> 00:28:55.160
You're handling this big
responsibility of hiring.

00:28:55.160 --> 00:28:57.300
And with collaboration
with Google,

00:28:57.300 --> 00:28:59.720
we were able to successfully
put in place some measures

00:28:59.720 --> 00:29:01.970
to make sure that even though
it was an alpha program,

00:29:01.970 --> 00:29:03.710
we had a successful launch.

00:29:03.710 --> 00:29:06.710
One of the things that
we found very helpful

00:29:06.710 --> 00:29:09.470
was actually having a customer
success function installed

00:29:09.470 --> 00:29:11.480
in this early-adoption program.

00:29:11.480 --> 00:29:14.750
So as we launched these jobs,
and J&amp;J being the second one,

00:29:14.750 --> 00:29:16.800
we were able to do it
about three times faster.

00:29:16.800 --> 00:29:19.660
So we got J&amp;J live, from the
time we got the thumbs up,

00:29:19.660 --> 00:29:20.990
in about a week.

00:29:20.990 --> 00:29:23.900
And going forward, we now
have a turnkey interface

00:29:23.900 --> 00:29:27.320
that any existing Jibe client
can be live on this API

00:29:27.320 --> 00:29:29.720
in under a day and
can begin testing.

00:29:29.720 --> 00:29:32.310
So it really gives us
the space to do that.

00:29:32.310 --> 00:29:34.079
But beyond that quick
turnaround time,

00:29:34.079 --> 00:29:35.870
we were able to actually
interface and make

00:29:35.870 --> 00:29:38.244
sure that the job families,
the job descriptions, the job

00:29:38.244 --> 00:29:40.516
application process,
were all maintained,

00:29:40.516 --> 00:29:42.890
and we could maximize that
value of that machine learning

00:29:42.890 --> 00:29:45.440
process and not have to
worry about production issues

00:29:45.440 --> 00:29:46.910
with such a young API.

00:29:46.910 --> 00:29:49.967
So it went very smoothly,
and we're two for two so far.

00:29:49.967 --> 00:29:51.800
MATTHEW MOORE: We hope
that trend continues.

00:29:51.800 --> 00:29:52.883
JOE ESSENFELD: Absolutely.

00:29:52.883 --> 00:29:55.580
MATTHEW MOORE: As you brought
Johnson &amp; Johnson and more

00:29:55.580 --> 00:29:57.860
companies going forward
online, has there

00:29:57.860 --> 00:30:01.670
been anything that surprised
you about hiring or the API

00:30:01.670 --> 00:30:02.277
in general?

00:30:02.277 --> 00:30:03.110
JOE ESSENFELD: Yeah.

00:30:03.110 --> 00:30:04.693
I think there were
two things, really,

00:30:04.693 --> 00:30:06.960
with J&amp;J that were surprising.

00:30:06.960 --> 00:30:10.540
The first one was how
important location search is.

00:30:10.540 --> 00:30:12.440
We were very focused
on understanding

00:30:12.440 --> 00:30:16.001
this story and the benefit
behind machine learning,

00:30:16.001 --> 00:30:17.750
talking about the
translation, and looking

00:30:17.750 --> 00:30:19.166
at the relevance
of search results

00:30:19.166 --> 00:30:21.230
like Sjoerd shared about
oncological research

00:30:21.230 --> 00:30:22.730
and microbiology.

00:30:22.730 --> 00:30:25.370
But when we started really
analyzing the query data,

00:30:25.370 --> 00:30:28.675
we noticed how often job
seekers searched for location.

00:30:28.675 --> 00:30:30.675
They searched for location
in the keyword field.

00:30:30.675 --> 00:30:32.716
They searched for location
in the location field.

00:30:32.716 --> 00:30:34.610
And the lift that
we saw-- at Jibe,

00:30:34.610 --> 00:30:37.190
we pride ourselves on having
good products all around,

00:30:37.190 --> 00:30:40.110
but our location search
didn't match Google's at all.

00:30:40.110 --> 00:30:41.702
It was more based on radius.

00:30:41.702 --> 00:30:43.910
And being from Manhattan
and working there, you know,

00:30:43.910 --> 00:30:45.770
when you do a radius search
for a job in Manhattan,

00:30:45.770 --> 00:30:47.270
you end up with
suggestions in Hoboken,

00:30:47.270 --> 00:30:48.530
and Jersey City, and Queens.

00:30:48.530 --> 00:30:50.990
And no offense to those
places, but people typically

00:30:50.990 --> 00:30:52.948
don't want to work there
when they're searching

00:30:52.948 --> 00:30:55.190
for a job in Manhattan.

00:30:55.190 --> 00:30:57.260
So we saw some great
lift with location alone

00:30:57.260 --> 00:31:00.860
and also the ability of the
API to parse out the results

00:31:00.860 --> 00:31:02.120
in the keyword field.

00:31:02.120 --> 00:31:04.220
So job seekers don't like
following directions.

00:31:04.220 --> 00:31:05.636
And when we looked
at the data, we

00:31:05.636 --> 00:31:07.460
saw the keyword field
was just polluted

00:31:07.460 --> 00:31:08.885
with all this information.

00:31:08.885 --> 00:31:11.510
And the machine learning API was
able to parse that out, return

00:31:11.510 --> 00:31:13.620
intelligent results back,
and improve conversion

00:31:13.620 --> 00:31:14.589
when we had that.

00:31:14.589 --> 00:31:16.130
So that was the
first surprise there.

00:31:16.130 --> 00:31:18.260
And then the second,
which was really funny,

00:31:18.260 --> 00:31:20.420
was this emotional
connection that people

00:31:20.420 --> 00:31:22.250
had understanding that
machine learning was

00:31:22.250 --> 00:31:24.140
powering the search.

00:31:24.140 --> 00:31:26.090
At first, it was
trying to debunk it.

00:31:26.090 --> 00:31:27.980
Oh, I'm going to
find the query that's

00:31:27.980 --> 00:31:29.660
going to show this
thing doesn't work.

00:31:29.660 --> 00:31:30.950
And then they're
pounding the API.

00:31:30.950 --> 00:31:32.060
They're looking for results.

00:31:32.060 --> 00:31:33.860
And then what they find
is how well it works,

00:31:33.860 --> 00:31:35.610
and they start to
understand that it's not

00:31:35.610 --> 00:31:38.030
about keyword
search, but it really

00:31:38.030 --> 00:31:40.850
goes back to Christian's
example about translation.

00:31:40.850 --> 00:31:44.254
And a good example was a query
that someone put in for SQL.

00:31:44.254 --> 00:31:46.170
So they were looking for
jobs related to that.

00:31:46.170 --> 00:31:48.026
And the first result that came
back-- and this wasn't for J&amp;J,

00:31:48.026 --> 00:31:50.839
it was for another client on the
API-- was an HR admin position.

00:31:50.839 --> 00:31:52.130
And they're like, oh, I got it.

00:31:52.130 --> 00:31:53.756
We found that this didn't work.

00:31:53.756 --> 00:31:55.130
And as they clicked
into the job,

00:31:55.130 --> 00:31:57.020
it wasn't in a
database category,

00:31:57.020 --> 00:31:59.810
wasn't in an IT category,
but at the bottom of the job

00:31:59.810 --> 00:32:01.790
description it mentioned
how important it was

00:32:01.790 --> 00:32:03.830
about pulling reports from SQL.

00:32:03.830 --> 00:32:07.250
So this was a great example
of how that keyword actually

00:32:07.250 --> 00:32:10.144
surfaced the skills
required for that job.

00:32:10.144 --> 00:32:11.810
And that person
switched, at that point,

00:32:11.810 --> 00:32:13.280
from trying to debunk
it to then trying

00:32:13.280 --> 00:32:15.655
to find more examples of how
this machine learning really

00:32:15.655 --> 00:32:16.190
worked.

00:32:16.190 --> 00:32:17.810
So those are two
great surprises as we

00:32:17.810 --> 00:32:19.102
implemented this and went live.

00:32:19.102 --> 00:32:20.060
MATTHEW MOORE: Awesome.

00:32:20.060 --> 00:32:22.040
We always love creating
more believers in it,

00:32:22.040 --> 00:32:24.680
so thanks for sharing.

00:32:24.680 --> 00:32:27.800
In the last few minutes
we have here on the stage,

00:32:27.800 --> 00:32:29.416
is there anything
else you might want

00:32:29.416 --> 00:32:31.040
to share with folks
in the audience who

00:32:31.040 --> 00:32:34.040
would be interested in improving
their hiring process and talent

00:32:34.040 --> 00:32:34.737
search?

00:32:34.737 --> 00:32:35.570
JOE ESSENFELD: Sure.

00:32:35.570 --> 00:32:38.240
I think one thing that's
really important to think about

00:32:38.240 --> 00:32:40.760
is job seekers as consumers.

00:32:40.760 --> 00:32:42.710
When they're on a
company's career site,

00:32:42.710 --> 00:32:44.750
that is when they're
the most engaged.

00:32:44.750 --> 00:32:46.970
And it really has to be
like any other transaction

00:32:46.970 --> 00:32:51.100
on an e-commerce site or any
other consumer-facing site.

00:32:51.100 --> 00:32:52.850
And when you think
about machine learning,

00:32:52.850 --> 00:32:55.610
it's important to think about
it in that consumer frame.

00:32:55.610 --> 00:32:57.380
So we want to find
the positions that

00:32:57.380 --> 00:32:59.732
are most strategic to
fill and understand

00:32:59.732 --> 00:33:01.940
how that translation of what
those job titles and job

00:33:01.940 --> 00:33:05.330
descriptions are are really
relevant to those job seekers.

00:33:05.330 --> 00:33:07.350
So I'd say take a step back.

00:33:07.350 --> 00:33:10.220
Don't think about quantity and
just filling your pipeline,

00:33:10.220 --> 00:33:11.900
but what's meaningful
to your company

00:33:11.900 --> 00:33:13.610
and how does that translation
through your machine learning

00:33:13.610 --> 00:33:15.420
really help improve
your conversion.

00:33:15.420 --> 00:33:16.910
And when you start thinking
about it in that context,

00:33:16.910 --> 00:33:18.743
you get a lot more
support and understanding

00:33:18.743 --> 00:33:20.330
inside the
organization about how

00:33:20.330 --> 00:33:22.460
to consumerize that
experience for job seekers

00:33:22.460 --> 00:33:24.742
and really improve
candidate experience.

00:33:24.742 --> 00:33:25.700
MATTHEW MOORE: Awesome.

00:33:25.700 --> 00:33:29.300
Well, again, thank you very
much for both joining us today

00:33:29.300 --> 00:33:31.070
and for being an
excellent partner

00:33:31.070 --> 00:33:33.710
through this alpha program
and hopefully beyond.

00:33:33.710 --> 00:33:35.870
In short, thank you so
much for taking the time

00:33:35.870 --> 00:33:39.080
to share your vision both for
Johnson &amp; Johnson and the API

00:33:39.080 --> 00:33:39.580
today.

00:33:39.580 --> 00:33:41.288
It's been great working
with both of you,

00:33:41.288 --> 00:33:43.297
and we hope to continue
it going forward.

00:33:43.297 --> 00:33:44.130
Thank you very much.

00:33:44.130 --> 00:33:44.450
SJOERD GEHRING: Thanks, Matt.

00:33:44.450 --> 00:33:44.820
JOE ESSENFELD: Thank you.

00:33:44.820 --> 00:33:45.474
Great.

00:33:45.474 --> 00:33:48.318
[APPLAUSE]

00:33:51.909 --> 00:33:52.950
MATTHEW MOORE: All right.

00:33:52.950 --> 00:33:57.600
So I'm sure you're all excited
to see what's next for the API.

00:33:57.600 --> 00:34:01.740
And today, we're pleased
to announce several updates

00:34:01.740 --> 00:34:06.390
to the alpha and share with
you some of our future plans

00:34:06.390 --> 00:34:08.630
for the API.

00:34:08.630 --> 00:34:11.360
We've been hard at work these
past few months evolving

00:34:11.360 --> 00:34:15.530
the Jobs API to address
additional customer needs

00:34:15.530 --> 00:34:19.340
and prepare the API for
broader adoption, which I'm

00:34:19.340 --> 00:34:21.880
sure you're all interested in.

00:34:21.880 --> 00:34:25.650
So first of all, we've
added featured jobs,

00:34:25.650 --> 00:34:29.639
which enables a hiring
company to promote jobs

00:34:29.639 --> 00:34:32.760
that it has a
business-critical need

00:34:32.760 --> 00:34:37.290
to fill as soon as possible and
be seen by as many job seekers

00:34:37.290 --> 00:34:40.520
as they can get it in front of.

00:34:40.520 --> 00:34:43.070
Secondly, we've
added histogramming,

00:34:43.070 --> 00:34:46.139
which is the ability to include
and render dynamic search

00:34:46.139 --> 00:34:49.489
counts alongside
your filters, which

00:34:49.489 --> 00:34:53.659
is going to greatly improve
the understandability

00:34:53.659 --> 00:34:57.250
of your search results
and experience.

00:34:57.250 --> 00:35:00.220
Next, we've added support
for bulk operations.

00:35:00.220 --> 00:35:04.750
Bulk operations allows you to
send multiple creates, updates,

00:35:04.750 --> 00:35:06.970
or deletes in the
same call, which

00:35:06.970 --> 00:35:09.910
is a much requested
and extremely useful

00:35:09.910 --> 00:35:12.730
feature when you're
managing a lot of job data

00:35:12.730 --> 00:35:15.910
like many of our customers do.

00:35:15.910 --> 00:35:18.790
And finally, we've
added significant

00:35:18.790 --> 00:35:21.310
relevance-thresholding
improvements

00:35:21.310 --> 00:35:25.480
further ensuring our objective
to return great matches

00:35:25.480 --> 00:35:28.750
for all job seeker
queries and showcase

00:35:28.750 --> 00:35:32.300
the search quality of the API.

00:35:32.300 --> 00:35:36.500
But now let's take a look
at what's coming next.

00:35:36.500 --> 00:35:38.950
So in the first
quarter of this year,

00:35:38.950 --> 00:35:41.230
we'll be rolling out
some important changes

00:35:41.230 --> 00:35:43.660
and enhancements to the API.

00:35:43.660 --> 00:35:46.060
To start, we're going to
be making improvements

00:35:46.060 --> 00:35:49.450
to how we handle locations
in your job data,

00:35:49.450 --> 00:35:51.790
improving our
support for both jobs

00:35:51.790 --> 00:35:54.700
without locations,
which happens, and jobs

00:35:54.700 --> 00:35:56.980
with many locations.

00:35:56.980 --> 00:36:00.220
This quarter, we're also going
to be introducing integrations

00:36:00.220 --> 00:36:02.800
for behavioral
data, improvements

00:36:02.800 --> 00:36:05.500
to our existing data
models, which will further

00:36:05.500 --> 00:36:09.140
improve your search results.

00:36:09.140 --> 00:36:11.840
Looking forward, this
half of the year,

00:36:11.840 --> 00:36:18.140
we're aiming to graduate our API
into beta, expanding and making

00:36:18.140 --> 00:36:22.710
the API prepared for
greater public availability.

00:36:22.710 --> 00:36:24.550
With the beta launch,
we're also aiming

00:36:24.550 --> 00:36:26.590
to release a couple
of cool new features,

00:36:26.590 --> 00:36:28.300
such as commute
search, which we're

00:36:28.300 --> 00:36:30.590
going to talk about
more in a moment,

00:36:30.590 --> 00:36:35.260
and job-to-job recommendations,
improving on our models, which

00:36:35.260 --> 00:36:40.790
will build smarter associations
between jobs in the system.

00:36:40.790 --> 00:36:43.030
And finally, as we
look to the future,

00:36:43.030 --> 00:36:46.540
we've got an ambitious
set of goals for the API

00:36:46.540 --> 00:36:50.920
to bring and expand the
API to customers worldwide,

00:36:50.920 --> 00:36:56.410
translating and localizing in
new countries and languages.

00:36:56.410 --> 00:36:59.380
And while we started
with job search,

00:36:59.380 --> 00:37:02.740
our goal is to take this
machine learning-powered API

00:37:02.740 --> 00:37:08.680
to the next level and enable
job-to-person recommendations,

00:37:08.680 --> 00:37:12.250
helping generate smart
job recommendations based

00:37:12.250 --> 00:37:17.560
on resumes and profiles, so
your job seekers never even

00:37:17.560 --> 00:37:20.330
have to search.

00:37:20.330 --> 00:37:24.350
Because search-- it's not always
the right tool for the job.

00:37:24.350 --> 00:37:27.900
It's limited to being
initiated by the job seeker.

00:37:27.900 --> 00:37:32.040
And this next step will
move the API forward,

00:37:32.040 --> 00:37:34.890
leveraging machine learning
to intelligently match

00:37:34.890 --> 00:37:39.830
job seekers with your
company's open opportunities.

00:37:39.830 --> 00:37:41.790
But let's take a moment
and dive into one

00:37:41.790 --> 00:37:44.190
of the features that I'm
extremely excited today

00:37:44.190 --> 00:37:45.900
to reveal for the
first time, which

00:37:45.900 --> 00:37:49.410
is the ability to search
jobs by how long it

00:37:49.410 --> 00:37:53.480
takes to commute to work.

00:37:53.480 --> 00:37:56.290
So as a little
background, it's been

00:37:56.290 --> 00:38:00.130
shown that an employee's
commute has one of the strongest

00:38:00.130 --> 00:38:03.120
ties to job satisfaction.

00:38:03.120 --> 00:38:06.860
And when you're trying to
attract and retain the best

00:38:06.860 --> 00:38:09.680
talent, employees
need to be satisfied

00:38:09.680 --> 00:38:13.550
with all aspects of their
job, including how long it

00:38:13.550 --> 00:38:16.630
takes to get to work.

00:38:16.630 --> 00:38:18.370
There are many job
seekers that depend

00:38:18.370 --> 00:38:21.070
on the availability of
timely public transportation

00:38:21.070 --> 00:38:22.360
to get to work.

00:38:22.360 --> 00:38:27.100
A job that's 20 miles away
may take over two hours

00:38:27.100 --> 00:38:30.070
to get to by bus, which
is time that could be

00:38:30.070 --> 00:38:32.590
spent at home with your family.

00:38:32.590 --> 00:38:35.560
An employee that has to spend
two hours commuting every day

00:38:35.560 --> 00:38:39.340
is probably going to
be an attrition risk.

00:38:39.340 --> 00:38:41.690
And even for drivers,
job search until now

00:38:41.690 --> 00:38:45.200
has been entirely straight
line distance-based.

00:38:45.200 --> 00:38:47.330
And that's useful
if you can afford

00:38:47.330 --> 00:38:49.880
to fly to work every day.

00:38:49.880 --> 00:38:53.120
Most job seekers don't have that
luxury or a straight-line route

00:38:53.120 --> 00:38:54.050
to work.

00:38:54.050 --> 00:38:56.700
And when you're commuting
during normal business hours,

00:38:56.700 --> 00:38:59.240
there's something that slows
you down even further, which

00:38:59.240 --> 00:39:00.380
is traffic.

00:39:00.380 --> 00:39:02.951
I'm sure you're all
familiar with it.

00:39:02.951 --> 00:39:05.120
With commute time
search we're aiming

00:39:05.120 --> 00:39:08.060
to help job seekers make
informed decisions around where

00:39:08.060 --> 00:39:11.780
their next job is located and
be happier, more effective

00:39:11.780 --> 00:39:12.990
employees.

00:39:12.990 --> 00:39:18.700
Let's take a look at the demo
and pray the internet works.

00:39:29.280 --> 00:39:29.780
All right.

00:39:29.780 --> 00:39:34.040
So we've loaded up this
quick demo running on the API

00:39:34.040 --> 00:39:37.970
to demonstrate how you might
use the API to allow job seekers

00:39:37.970 --> 00:39:39.710
to search by commute time.

00:39:39.710 --> 00:39:42.410
On the right, you'll see the
browser developer console,

00:39:42.410 --> 00:39:44.792
which is showing the
real-time API output.

00:39:44.792 --> 00:39:47.000
And we've loaded this instance
up with some test data

00:39:47.000 --> 00:39:48.320
for software
engineering positions,

00:39:48.320 --> 00:39:50.210
where you can specify
a street address, which

00:39:50.210 --> 00:39:54.090
is Sunnyvale in this case,
a method of transportation,

00:39:54.090 --> 00:39:57.660
which is either public
transit, walking, or driving--

00:39:57.660 --> 00:39:59.980
and we'll pick driving here--

00:39:59.980 --> 00:40:02.320
and the amount of time
you're willing to commute,

00:40:02.320 --> 00:40:05.990
which we'll leave at 30
minutes in this case.

00:40:05.990 --> 00:40:10.240
When we search, we can
see the API returns jobs

00:40:10.240 --> 00:40:12.130
that have street
addresses specifically

00:40:12.130 --> 00:40:15.220
reachable within 30 minutes,
as indicated by the blue area

00:40:15.220 --> 00:40:16.940
on the map.

00:40:16.940 --> 00:40:20.800
And as you can see, for
this job in particular

00:40:20.800 --> 00:40:24.070
we were able to identify
the company's street address

00:40:24.070 --> 00:40:27.760
and determine that this job
is 28 minutes away in traffic.

00:40:30.390 --> 00:40:33.000
To take a look at
another quick example,

00:40:33.000 --> 00:40:35.480
let's search from an address
in the city of San Francisco--

00:40:35.480 --> 00:40:39.730
say, the downtown area,
where we all are right now.

00:40:39.730 --> 00:40:41.570
And because I'm
commuting from the city,

00:40:41.570 --> 00:40:44.660
I'm more interested in
public transit options.

00:40:44.660 --> 00:40:46.509
And I live in San Francisco.

00:40:46.509 --> 00:40:48.050
I have a high
tolerance for commute--

00:40:48.050 --> 00:40:51.130
say, 60 minutes or so.

00:40:51.130 --> 00:40:57.140
When I search, I can see the
extremely non-polygonal set

00:40:57.140 --> 00:41:00.350
of locations, where we return
jobs which are influenced

00:41:00.350 --> 00:41:01.970
by local public
transport options--

00:41:01.970 --> 00:41:04.580
BART, which takes you up through
Walnut Creek, and Caltrain,

00:41:04.580 --> 00:41:07.300
which takes you down
into the peninsula.

00:41:07.300 --> 00:41:10.150
And we can get an accurate
and specific understanding

00:41:10.150 --> 00:41:12.910
of where I can reach in an
hour from the city, which

00:41:12.910 --> 00:41:17.500
is very important if I don't
have a car, for example.

00:41:17.500 --> 00:41:19.290
You know, this is
a brand new way

00:41:19.290 --> 00:41:23.460
of representing location-based
job search, one that's

00:41:23.460 --> 00:41:29.880
more reflective of the actual
experience of traveling

00:41:29.880 --> 00:41:31.240
between home and work.

00:41:34.850 --> 00:41:38.419
Sorry, just jumping
back to the slides here.

00:41:38.419 --> 00:41:40.210
We're excited to share
this with you today,

00:41:40.210 --> 00:41:43.680
and that's your sneak peek
at the future of the Jobs

00:41:43.680 --> 00:41:46.500
API, which you can look
forward to seeing more about

00:41:46.500 --> 00:41:48.900
in the next couple of months.

00:41:48.900 --> 00:41:52.317
We hope you enjoyed this first
look at this brand new feature,

00:41:52.317 --> 00:41:54.900
and we're excited to be making
this available to our customers

00:41:54.900 --> 00:41:57.889
going forward in
the near future.

00:41:57.889 --> 00:42:00.180
And we really can't wait to
see what awesome stuff they

00:42:00.180 --> 00:42:03.162
bring to the table with them.

00:42:03.162 --> 00:42:04.158
[APPLAUSE]

00:42:04.158 --> 00:42:05.154
Thank you.

00:42:11.959 --> 00:42:13.750
You're probably all
wondering at this point

00:42:13.750 --> 00:42:16.450
how to get started
with the Jobs API.

00:42:16.450 --> 00:42:18.940
If you manage a
company's career site--

00:42:18.940 --> 00:42:22.480
and we already have a number of
approved reseller integrations

00:42:22.480 --> 00:42:24.280
in place at this time--

00:42:24.280 --> 00:42:27.160
we recommend you contact your
applicant tracking system

00:42:27.160 --> 00:42:31.420
or career site provider to ask
about leveraging the Jobs API.

00:42:31.420 --> 00:42:33.630
And if they're not
already a reseller,

00:42:33.630 --> 00:42:36.130
encourage them to sign up with
Google at the website you see

00:42:36.130 --> 00:42:42.330
here, cloud.google.com/jobs-API.

00:42:42.330 --> 00:42:45.165
For anyone here that runs an
applicant tracking system,

00:42:45.165 --> 00:42:48.780
or is a career site
provider, or a job board,

00:42:48.780 --> 00:42:51.690
or any other business interested
in leveraging the API,

00:42:51.690 --> 00:42:55.140
please also visit
cloud.google.com/jobs-API

00:42:55.140 --> 00:42:57.780
to register your
interest in the service,

00:42:57.780 --> 00:43:00.400
and we'll get back
to you shortly.

00:43:00.400 --> 00:43:02.840
Our API is still in alpha.

00:43:02.840 --> 00:43:04.900
And once we've granted
you access, getting

00:43:04.900 --> 00:43:07.480
started with the API is
as simple as setting up

00:43:07.480 --> 00:43:09.820
a new project in the
Cloud developer console

00:43:09.820 --> 00:43:12.490
and registering your
account with our service.

